<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The theme of the WINDOW, which has nothing to do with the Compose theme and which this module
      did not have. That absence is what made the first-launch notice screen unreadable.

      ### What Android picks when you do not pick

      Without `android:theme` on `<application>`, the system does not use a neutral theme: it
      applies `Theme.DeviceDefault.Light.DarkActionBar` (`Resources.selectDefaultTheme`, as soon as
      `targetSdk` exceeds 17). The name says exactly what we were getting — a **light**
      `windowBackground` (measured: #F0EDEF on the device, tinted by Material You) topped by a
      **dark** action bar carrying "Pendulum".

      Compose does not paint a background on its own. Everywhere the composition painted one — the
      `Scaffold` of `PendulumNavHost`, whose `containerColor` is `colorScheme.background` — the
      light background was covered and nothing showed. First-launch onboarding, however, is a plain
      `Column`: the light window background stayed visible under text written in the dark palette
      (#E6EAF0), that is, a contrast of 1.06:1. Unreadable, on the screen that carries "this is not
      a medical device".

      ### Why this file and not just a Compose fix

      `PendulumTheme` now paints its background, which would be enough for display. Two things are
      only repaired here:

      - **The first frame.** The window is drawn before a single line of Compose runs. A light
        `windowBackground` produces a white flash at every cold start, that is, at 7 in the morning,
        in the dark — exactly what the choice of a dark theme exists to avoid (`06-interface.md`
        section 5.4).
      - **The action bar.** It is in no mock-up, it repeats the name of the application already
        present in the launcher, and it eats 56 dp of height on screens of long text. `NoActionBar`
        removes it; the product's navigation is the Compose one.

      A consequence to hold to: without an action bar, nothing in the decor reserves room for the
      system bars any more. It is `Scaffold` that reserves it on the screens of `PendulumNavHost`,
      and `OnboardingPager` that reserves it explicitly for onboarding.

      The background value is that of `PendulumColors.Dark.background`, and `TextContrastTest`
      fails the compilation of the tests if the two diverge: two sources for a single colour is
      exactly the defect being repaired.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.Pendulum" parent="@android:style/Theme.DeviceDefault.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#FF0E1116</item>
    </style>
</resources>
